\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The charge test at one site}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On a finite periodic grid with nonzero finite-dimensional
complex Hermitian values #{S\otimes F}, choose self-adjoint
Euclidean directions #{\varepsilon_j} on #{S}, a
\vocabk{charge conjugation of sign}{fdip-017H} #{c_\eta} and
a fiber antiunitary #{c_F:F\to F}. The sitewise value map
#{\mathcal Q=c_\eta\otimes c_F} conjugates a represented
positive link to #{U_j^{\mathcal Q}(x)=c_F U_j(x)c_F^{-1}}.
For #{n=2m\ge2} it exchanges the sign spaces of the canonical
grading #{C=\ui^m\psI} exactly when #{m} is odd. For another
constant self-adjoint \vocabk{grading}{fdip-013N} #{\Gamma}
the exchange condition is #{\mathcal Q\Gamma\mathcal Q^{-1}=-\Gamma}.}

\p{Suppose the sign spaces are exchanged. Specify a group
identification #{\alpha:G\to G'} with the destination action
#{\rho'} pulled back along it, or use the conjugated action
#{\rho'(g)=\mathcal Q\rho(g)\mathcal Q^{-1}} of the same
abstract group. Assume the source and destination actions are
unitary and preserve their sign spaces. Then #{\mathcal Q}
intertwines the given actions exactly when both restrictions
#{u_\pm=\mathcal Q|_{V_\pm}:V_\pm\to V_\mp} satisfy
##{u_\pm\rho_\pm(g)=\rho'_\mp(\alpha(g))u_\pm\qquad(g\in G),}
using #{\alpha=\mathrm{id}} for the conjugated action.
With destination scalar data #{\phi'} and even onsite blocks
as in \ref{fdip-018H}, the coefficient test is, for both signs,
##{u_\pm A_\pm(x;U,\phi)
 = A'_\mp(x;U^{\mathcal Q},\phi')u_\pm\qquad\text{at every site } x.}}

\proof{
\p{The map on simple tensors conjugates a scalar on either
factor, so it respects the tensor balancing relation. The
product inner product is conjugated and the two inverses give
an inverse on values. Acting sitewise leaves endpoints in
place and conjugates the positive link on its fiber, giving
the stated transport. In #{C} the product #{\psI} consists of
all #{2m} directions in their prescribed order. The generator
signs contribute #{\eta^{2m}=1} and conjugating the coefficient
#{\ui^m} contributes #{(-1)^m}. Thus the sign in
#{\mathcal Q C\mathcal Q^{-1}=(-1)^m C} agrees with
\ref{fdip-017P}. Since #{C} is an involution on nonzero
values, its preservation and negation are distinct.}

\p{The two eigenvalues of #{\Gamma} are real, so negating the
grading is equivalent to mapping both sign spaces onto their
opposites. On each of those spaces apply the definition of
[equivariant sector exchange](fdip-018A) to its group action.
For the conjugated action the displayed group equations hold
by construction. The source and destination coefficient
operators act at the same site. Their two required
intertwining equations follow by restricting as in
\ref{fdip-018F}, and conversely they give the identity on
every field by addition.}
}
